In article <2443@hydra.gatech.EDU> ce1zzes@prism.gatech.EDU (Eric Sheppard) writes:
>
>I got another vicious type of bug, related to file/level locking.  On a Sun
>running 4.3, people die randomly by trickery.  Seems that if several people
>play at once, then dying players trash the Xlock.x files of other players.

	This sounds like a problem I fixed way back in V2.2 (and my fix
is still in the V3 code!  Wow!  A contribution to posterity!).  You have
a playground on a Sun, and players on other machines mounting it over NFS,
yes?  You need to compile with "NETWORK" defined in unixconf.h to fix it.

	The problem is that one of the ways hack looks to see if a lock
file is "stale" is that lock files have the pid of the game process in
them; new hacks starting up check for the existence of the old process
and reuse the lock file name if it's gone.  Of course, with NFS the process
might be alive and well, but living on another machine...

